// Premier Caterers — Firestore security rules
//
// STEP 1 (this file): require an identity.
// Every app now signs in before it touches the database — staff with their
// real login, everyone else silently and anonymously. So the database can
// stop accepting requests from anyone at all on the internet.
//
// What this stops: someone finding the database address in the public
// source and reading or deleting client invoices, contracts, time-clock
// punches and vendor pricing straight from a browser console. That is
// exactly what "if true" allowed.
//
// What this does NOT stop: someone who reads the public source, gets their
// own anonymous token, and then asks for data. This is a locked door, not
// a guard — a big improvement, not the finished job.
//
// STEP 2 (later): per-collection rules. Invoices, contracts, clientPortals,
// events and the tc_* payroll collections restricted to real staff logins;
// clients able to create a drop-off order or an inquiry but never to read
// anyone else's. That needs testing app by app and is its own project.
//
// BEFORE CHANGING THIS FILE: every app must already be deployed with the
// identity gate. Any app without it stops working the moment these rules
// go live.

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
    }

  }
}
